<!--
  /styling — sx token utilities, host + variant styling, and the token
  convention. Styling lives on the element as sx utilities; raw <style> is
  the escape hatch for what sx structurally can't express.
-->
<column class="doc styling-doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guides</p>
  <h1>Styling</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI components style themselves with <code>sx</code> — token utilities
    written right on the element. No stylesheet to coordinate, no CSS-in-JS
    runtime, no class names to invent. <code>sx</code> is a closed
    vocabulary: every value is a design token, so a component is themed by
    construction.
  </p>

  <h2>The <code>sx</code> attribute</h2>
  <p>
    Put styling on the element as an <code>sx</code> attribute — space-separated
    <code>property:token</code> pairs. The compiler lowers each pair to an
    atomic rule at build time; nothing is computed at runtime. Because the
    vocabulary is closed, there is no pixel value like <code>p:16px</code> and
    no hex like <code>bg:#fff</code> — only tokens resolve.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.sx}></code-block>
  <p>
    Text is already styled: write <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>–<code>&lt;h6&gt;</code>
    and <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> plain and they take the type scale. Reach for
    <code>sx</code> when you need surface, spacing, or tone.
  </p>

  <h2>Styling the host</h2>
  <p>
    To style the component's own element — its border, radius, background —
    use <code>&lt;host sx&gt;</code>. It is the <code>.sui-page</code> rule,
    expressed in the same token vocabulary as everything else.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.host}></code-block>

  <h2>State with <code>&lt;variants&gt;</code></h2>
  <p>
    State-driven styling keys off the host's attributes. A
    <code>&lt;variants&gt;</code> block repaints a class when the host
    carries an attribute — <code>[open]</code>, <code>[disabled]</code>,
    a <code>tone</code> value — without a controller writing styles by hand.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.variants}></code-block>

  <h2>Tokens, not literals</h2>
  <p>
    Because <code>sx</code> only speaks tokens, theming is automatic and dark
    mode is free. A literal — a hex color, a pixel size, a fixed font — would
    defeat theming and lock the component to one brand voice. Pick the token
    by intent across the four layers: semantic surfaces, tone ramps, the
    brand palette, and the primitive scale. A card sits on
    <code>bg:surface-1</code>; a status alert reads
    <code>bg:tone-warning-bg</code>; a headline reads
    <code>text:xl</code>.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.tokens}></code-block>

  <h2>Status tones, not palette colors</h2>
  <p>
    Tone ramps are status surfaces, not brand colors. <code>success</code>
    is the right token for a positive confirmation; <code>primary</code> is a
    brand action. Mixing the two breaks every theme that ships its own
    success color.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.tone}></code-block>

  <h2>Layout is keywords</h2>
  <p>
    Structure comes from <code>&lt;column&gt;</code>,
    <code>&lt;row&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;grid&gt;</code>, and
    <code>&lt;center&gt;</code> — never a raw <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or a
    hand-rolled flexbox. The layout keywords encode the spacing scale, the
    breakpoints, and the responsive defaults, so <code>gap="3"</code> looks
    the same wherever it's reused. Raw containers are a lint error, not a
    style choice.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.layout}></code-block>

  <h2>The escape hatch</h2>
  <p>
    Some things <code>sx</code> structurally can't express: pseudo-elements,
    <code>::slotted</code>, <code>grid-template-columns</code>,
    <code>top</code>/<code>left</code>, <code>accent-color</code>. For those,
    a raw <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block is the honest escape hatch — still
    scoped to the shadow root, still reading tokens. Keep it small: if a rule
    <em>can</em> be an <code>sx</code> atom, it should be.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.escape}></code-block>

  <row class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/theming">← Theming</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/composition">Composition →</a>
  </row>
</column>
